To simplify notation, suppose that $A$ is a $3 \times 3$ matrix with eigenvalues $a \geq b \geq c$. Arrange the (eigenvector) columns of $P$ so that $P = \begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{u}_{1} & \textbf{u}_{2} & \textbf{u}_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}$ and

example.paragraph:
The constrained maximum of $\textbf{x}^{T}A\textbf{x}$ is attained when $\textbf{x}$ is a unit eigenvector for $\text{λ} = 6$. Solve $(A - 6I)\textbf{x} = 0$ and find an eigenvector $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Set $\textbf{u}_{1} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{3}} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{3}} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{3}}
      \end{bmatrix}$.

and the utility function becomes $q(3x_{1},2x_{2}) = (3x_{1})(2x_{2}) = 6x_{1}x_{2}$. Let $\textbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Then the problem is to maximize $Q(\textbf{x}) = 6x_{1}x_{2}$ subject to $\textbf{x}^{T}\textbf{x} = 1$. Note that $Q(\textbf{x}) = \textbf{x}^{T}A\textbf{x}$, where

The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\pm 3$, with eigenvectors $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ for $\text{λ} = 3$ and $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ for $\text{λ} = - 3$. Thus the maximum value of $Q(\textbf{x}) = q(x_{1},x_{2})$ is $3$, attained when $x_{1} = 1\text{/}\sqrt{2}$ and $x_{2} = 1\text{/}\sqrt{2}$.
